\end{table}

\textbf{Low-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): per-seed consistent gaming.}
ISR reward increases TS rates in all 3 seeds relative to matched random: $70.6\%$ ($163/231$) vs.\ $13.7\%$ ($36/262$) pooled (supplementary CMH: $\text{OR}_{\text{CMH}}{=}2.46$, $p{=}5.6{\times}10^{-16}$; Appendix~\ref{app:statistical-details}). %% registry: plan002 vs plan018 all seeds; D095 FINAL
Random TS is seed-dependent ($38.3\%$ in s43 vs.\ $0\%$ in s42/s44); ISR makes TS near-deterministic ($2/3$ seeds at $97$--$100\%$, Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}).
TS (a single premise identical to the conclusion, giving $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ by construction; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}) is the canonical Goodhart move: the model satisfies the LOO definition directly.
Total conclusion embedding ($\text{CE}+\text{TS}$) is $68$--$100\%$ across both conditions; the composition differs: CE dominates under random ($71\%$), TS under ISR. ISR converts CE into TS rather than reducing total embedding (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}).
Random yields higher PR ($87{\pm}8\%$ vs.\ $77{\pm}14\%$): noise disrupts convergence toward a single collapse trajectory.

\textbf{Premise-count confound.}
The sole non-TS ISR seed (s42) shows raw $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.415$, $3.2\times$ random ($0.131$), a premise-minimization artifact ($n{=}5.58$ vs.\ $8.46$); matched comparison gives $1.06\times$ (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% D051 premise-count-matched analysis
After reclassifying PG and TS false negatives, non-hacking rates ($6.1\%$ ISR vs.\ $9.5\%$ random) are not significant ($p{=}0.182$; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). %% D095 FINAL Fisher test
The key asymmetry is compositional: ISR converts stochastic TS into near-deterministic convergence ($2/3$ seeds ${\geq}97\%$), while random remains seed-dependent and CE-dominated.

\textbf{Higher doses and temporal dynamics.}
ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ non-hacking is transient: seed~42 drops $17.3\%{\to}2.2\%$ by 1000 steps as TS escalates to $96.7\%$ (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}). %% registry: plan002-b01-s42-1000, plan016-extended-1000-s42
At higher doses ($\beta{\geq}0.2$), TS becomes universal ($0\%$ non-hacking at $\beta{=}0.2$); $\beta{=}0.3$ destabilizes both ISR ($\text{PR}{=}66{\pm}42\%$) and random ($54{\pm}35\%$), with ISR producing multi-modal failure~\citep{gao-reward-overoptimization} and random converging to CE ($91\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:beta03}). %% registry: plan002-additive-b02-s42/s43/s44, plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44

\subsection{Cross-Domain Evidence}
\label{sec:crossdomain}
\label{sec:crosslogic}
\label{sec:crossdataset}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Top}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$; $^\dagger$s43 FOL uses sampling $T{=}0.7$, not strictly comparable). \textbf{Bottom}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition; all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Prolog: universal Goodhart.}
We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}).
Exec-only seeds show $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0$ via redundant ground-fact coding (2/3 seeds); ISR flags this mode as it flags FOL's conclusion embedding.
Under ISR-as-reward, 3/3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms: clean TS (s42), uppercase pseudo-Prolog bypassing text-match classifiers (s43, all 38 proved at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$), and query-as-fact gaming (s44, 95/97 at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$; Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}).
Prolog's simpler semantics broadens the gaming basin, eliminating the non-hacking residual entirely.

\textbf{ProofFOL exec-only: structural degeneration generalizes, though mode distributions differ.}
Two converged seeds ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.07$) show ${\leq}1.4\%$ genuine; s42 exhibits 100\% TS (absent from all FOLIO exec-only seeds), showing hacking-mode repertoire is dataset-specific ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$ across all seeds, far below baseline $0.31$).

\textbf{ProofFOL ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): beneficial instability (exploratory).}
ISR reduces stable convergence from 2/3 to 0/3 seeds, yet ISR seeds average 76.8\% genuine versus 17.4\% for exec-only ($p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$; Table~\ref{tab:prooffol-detailed}).
ProofFOL ``genuine'' excludes only TS and CE (the two modes detectable by text-match classifiers), unlike FOLIO's eight-mode residual; the rates are therefore not directly comparable.
However, ISR-condition prove rates ($0.35$--$0.49$) remain far below exec-only (${\sim}1.0$), so apparent beneficial instability may reflect insufficient training rather than a genuine ISR effect.

\textbf{Mechanism.}
On ProofFOL, hacking yields $\text{ISR}{=}0.0$ while genuine proofs yield $\text{ISR}{>}0.2$; ISR correctly penalizes shortcuts.
On FOLIO, $P{\to}P$ yields $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ (regressional Goodhart~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}), so ISR erroneously rewards tautological hacking.
The same metric produces opposite outcomes, determined by whether the dataset's hacking geometry satisfies it.

